# Raw stores stay out of git; COMPACT BUNDLES go in. The distinction is size and
# reproducibility, not principle. candles/ is ~1.2GB of minute bars and is regenerable
# from the exchange; candle-bundle/ is ~9MB of daily bars and is NOT -- Kraken's public
# OHLC endpoint returns only 720 rows, so a lost bundle cannot be re-pulled, only
# rebuilt from a raw store that exists on one machine.
# It was excluded here until 2026-09-10, when a container restart destroyed it along
# with the uploads directory and took 18 tests down with it. sp500-bundle (11M),
# candle-bundle-long (4.3M) and funding-binance (5.8M) were already tracked, so this
# rule was the only thing standing between the crypto universe and permanence.
candle-bundle.tar.gz

# Journals are decision records rather than source, and the argument against committing one is
# real: it mixes a run's history into the repository's. THE PAPER JOURNAL IS TRACKED ANYWAY, and
# the reasoning is the same one that put the compact bundles in here above.
#
# It is the deliverable of the whole paper month. Untracked, it lived in whichever container or
# machine happened to run `paper`, and this container is reclaimed after a period of inactivity --
# so the first evidence-grade record this project ever produced would be deleted by a timeout, and
# `settle` on any later run would find nothing to settle. A record that cannot survive the week is
# not a record. A mixed history is the smaller cost, and it is reversible; a deleted month is not.
#
# ONE MACHINE SHOULD APPEND TO IT. Two machines both appending produce a genuine merge conflict in
# an append-only file -- resolvable by taking the union of the lines, but easier not to cause.
*.jsonl
!analyst-journal.jsonl

# The news cache is fetched per-deployment from a Gateway, and a committed one would be
# both stale and a point-in-time record of somebody else's run.
data/news-cache.json
